// result_end
\caption(Actual duplicate/context controls and retained equal-key spellings.)
\alttext(Punctuation can affect native context even when it emits no key byte.)

> Verification and references

Native tests retain all twelve original keys and independently hand-check20
context fixtures, including digraphs, final mb, duplicate cc and retained
original Catherine/Kathryn equality. They inspect actual normalized source
spans/lookahead flags/emitted suffixes and counts for19608 short rule-alphabet
words, every16384 ASCII byte pair and1000 arbitrary-byte strings. Empty input,
NUL, actual onset removal, ownership and exact quota boundaries are covered.
Existing phonetic regression tests remain. The shared ten-report batch passes 71
CTest cases, 29 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 130 compiled and
executed public examples, 598/598 scoped documentation definitions and ten
representative hovers. Independent Python arbitrary-precision, hashlib,
sorted-window, RNG, polynomial, graph and full-assignment references verify
224,229 actual records. Frozen comparisons preserve ordinary legacy outputs,
including selected clique identities, original density graphs and memetic
partitions. Twelve checkout headers and twelve identically installed headers
compile independently; an installed-header native consumer links and runs. All
four complete programs and all four native figures pass execution and visual
review, and HTML/Markdown exports preserve computed results and identical image
bytes. Receipts: `build/mepml-conversion/batch-224-233/`.

Philips (1990), *Hanging on the Metaphone*, Computer Language7(12),
December1990, is the historical method reference. This report specifies the
retained datamunge variant and does not claim equivalence to every
implementation or repeat an unexecuted corpus-validation claim from the legacy
report.

The [Metaphone implementation archive|https://aspell.sourceforge.net/metaphone/]
identifies the December1990 origin and links original code and multiple modified
implementations. The native byte conventions above identify the version examined
here.
